--- Finding (solve_a_numbrix_puzzle): a list of growing ints (items read by
--- `int[text]`, which always answers the growing form) written a whole-number
--- literal by position (`cells[0] = 7`) is an internal error in both modes, before
--- anything runs.
---
--- crow run -i and crow run alike (exit 1, nothing printed):
---   solve_a_numbrix_puzzle-1.co: internal error: the lowered program disagrees
---   with itself — a bug in Crow, not in this program: solve_a_numbrix_puzzle-1
---   main: line 24: an item of 'cells', a list of Big, is assigned Int
--- Expected: [5] then [7] in both modes (the literal widened to the list's kind).
---
--- The same inside a function where the list is read from a record's field:
---     struct P [cells]
---     fn first_set[p]
---         board = p.cells
---         board[0] = 1          --- "fn first_set: line 5: an item of 'board', a
---         return board          ---  list of Big, is assigned Int"
---     log[first_set[P[push[[], int['5']]]]]
--- A parameter written so (`fn f[cells]` / `cells[0] = 1`) works, and so does a
--- local copy of a parameter (`board = cells`). Worked around in the solution by
--- writing the boards as lists of whole numbers instead of text read by `int`.
cells = push[[], int['5']]
log[cells]
cells[0] = 7
log[cells]
